STEP 0 -- baseline: configure the mirror this case will remove             done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the case needs a mirror to exist before it can be removed. Direction `both` was
  chosen as the most complete form, so removal is not tested against a partial case.

x960-56YSQ#configure terminal
Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.11
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.10 direction both
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#end
                                           <-- accepted: no output, no error line

Pre-removal state -- the thing that must afterwards be gone:

x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.11
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: both
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.10

x960-56YSQ#show interface status
  Port        Name               Status      Vlan      Duplex Speed   Type
  ...
  port1.0.10  -                  notconnect          1 auto   auto    not present
  port1.0.11  -                  notconnect          1 auto   auto    not present
                                           <-- the destination port reads EXACTLY the same with
                                               a mirror configured as without one. Unlike a
                                               stackport, a mirror destination is not labelled
                                               here, so this output cannot evidence the port
                                               leaving or returning to normal switching.
                                               Compare the identical reading in STEP 3.

STEP 1 -- "remove the mirror" (as drafted: `no mirror interface`)          FAIL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: sent exactly as the case was drafted, with no argument.

x960-56YSQ#configure terminal
Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.11
  % port1.0.11 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
                                           <-- ADVISORY, not a rejection: the command succeeded
                                               and the prompt advanced to (config-if)#
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface
  % Incomplete command.                    <-- a GENUINE rejection

The DUT's own help had already predicted this, read with `?` and no trailing CR:

x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface ?
  IFRANGE  Source Interface to use
  none     No source interface (set destination interface only)
                                           <-- no `<cr>`, so an argument is mandatory

  => STEP 1 FAIL: `no mirror interface` with no argument is not a valid command on the x960.
     This step fails ONLY because the drafted command is wrong; it says nothing about the
     platform's ability to remove a mirror, which STEP 2 demonstrates. A rejected command
     configures nothing, so the device state was unchanged.

STEP 2 -- "remove the mirror" (the form the DUT takes)                     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#end
                                           <-- accepted: no output, no error line

  => STEP 2 PASS: the mirror is removed by naming its source interface.

STEP 3 -- "verify the mirror is gone and left no residue"                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
x960-56YSQ#
                                           <-- EMPTY: the mirror is no longer listed

x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.11
  !
  interface port1.0.11
   switchport
   switchport mode access
  !
                                           <-- the `mirror interface port1.0.10 direction both`
                                               line is gone; a plain access port again

x960-56YSQ#show interface status
  Port        Name               Status      Vlan      Duplex Speed   Type
  ...
  port1.0.10  -                  notconnect          1 auto   auto    not present
  port1.0.11  -                  notconnect          1 auto   auto    not present
                                           <-- byte-for-byte identical to STEP 0, which is why
                                               it is not used as evidence either way

  => STEP 3 PASS: removal is proven by the two discriminating readings -- `show mirror` is empty
     and the mirror line is absent from the port's running-config. The claim that the port
     "returns to normal switching" holds as a configuration state only, for the reasons in
     CAVEAT.
